course: medicine
lesson: 71
title: Swimming out too far: sea, rivers, lakes and quarries
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Kai, 10, on a Cornwall beach paddles out past the red-and-yellow flags: «Them flags are for babies, our kid»; two minutes later the beach looks small, because outside the flags is where rip currents often run, strong channels of water flowing out to sea, and no lifeguard is watching that water.
explain: Every summer children drown in UK seas, rivers, lakes and quarries, often on hot sunny days and often close to the shore; it happens fast and quietly, and it can't be undone, so the rules here are not for babies, they are the reason those children aren't you.
explain: UK open water stays cold even in summer, and sudden cold makes you gasp and breathe wildly (cold water shock); the RLSS (Royal Life Saving Society) advice «Float to Live» is to lean back, ears in the water, arms and legs spread, until the gasping passes in about a minute or a minute and a half.
explain: A drowning person usually can't shout or wave; they bob quietly with the mouth at the water line, so if someone in the water looks wrong, you shout for a lifeguard or adult straight away.
explain: If someone is in trouble, you never go in after them: Call 999 (at the coast ask for the Coastguard), Tell them to lean back and float, Throw something that floats, all from the bank.
fact: Lifeguards watch the water between the red-and-yellow flags; outside them nobody may be watching.
fact: A rip current is a strong channel of water flowing away from the beach; it pulls you out, not under, people drown when they tire and chill fighting it, and a strong rip can move about as fast as an Olympic swimmer.
fact: In a rip, don't swim straight against it: if you can stand, wade; if not, float, raise one hand and shout for help.
fact: UK seas, lakes, rivers and quarries stay cold all year, and sunny air does not mean warm water.
fact: Cold water shock can start in water below about 15 °C: you gasp and can't control your breathing, and if your head is under you can breathe in water.
fact: The gasping from cold water shock usually passes in about 60 to 90 seconds, and floating on your back keeps your face up while it does.
fact: Flooded quarries are often very deep and very cold, with steep sides you can't climb out on and old machinery under the surface; bright blue water can come from minerals and hides the danger.
fact: Fast-moving water can knock you off your feet even below the knee, and riverbeds are slippery.
fact: A drowning person is usually silent, head tipped back, mouth at the water line, and a child can go under in well under a minute.
fact: People who jump in to rescue someone often drown themselves; the RLSS advice is Call 999, Tell them to float, Throw something that floats.
fact: At the coast, call 999 and ask for the Coastguard; wind blowing off the land can push inflatables far out to sea in minutes.
fact: The tide comes in across flat sand faster than you can walk and fills channels behind you first, cutting off sandbanks.
fact: The old rule «no swimming for an hour after lunch» isn't backed by good evidence; cold, currents, going out too far and swimming unwatched are what kill.
fact: «Grand» in Ireland means fine; «lush» is Welsh and West Country slang for lovely; «bruv» is London slang for mate.
exercise: Where should you swim at a lifeguarded beach? | Between the red-and-yellow flags | Anywhere the waves look biggest | Just outside the flags, by the rocks | Out past the lifeguards' view
exercise: What does a rip current do? | Pulls you out to sea, away from the beach | Pulls you straight down to the bottom of the sea | Pushes you back onto the sand | Spins you round in one spot
exercise: Aoife is in a rip: «I'll just swim back hard». What's the advice? | Don't fight it: float, raise a hand and shout | Swim straight back against it as hard as you can | Dive under it and swim along the bottom | Take off your swimsuit to swim faster
exercise: Can a top swimmer always beat a rip? | No, a rip can be as fast as Olympic swimmers | Yes, good swimmers can beat any current there is | Yes, if they swim with their eyes shut | No, but a 9-year-old can
exercise: Lucas jumps into a lake on a hot June day and can't speak. Why? | Cold water shock makes him gasp | The sun has burnt his throat | Lakes are warm and made him sleepy | He swallowed a fish
exercise: Below about what water temperature can cold water shock start? | About 15 °C | About 40 °C | About 30 °C | Only below 0 °C
exercise: Priya falls off a paddleboard and panics. What should she do? | Lean back and float until the gasping passes | Thrash hard to reach the bottom | Hold her breath and sink to rest | Swim fast for the far bank at once, before she tires
exercise: How long does the cold-shock gasping usually last? | About 60 to 90 seconds | About 10 minutes or more | About 2 hours, until she warms up | About 2 seconds, just a quick gasp
exercise: Why is a flooded quarry dangerous? | Deep, very cold, steep sides and hidden junk | The water is warm and too salty | It is always full of sharks | The blue colour stings your eyes
exercise: Seán wades into the river after his ball. What's the rule? | Let the ball go and tell an adult | Wade in only up to the knees | Run in fast before it floats off | Swim after it if it's not too far
exercise: How does a drowning person usually look? | Quiet, bobbing, mouth at the water line | Splashing wildly and screaming loudly for help | Waving both arms over the head and calling out | Laughing and diving under the water again
exercise: Oliver's mate slips into the canal. What should Oliver do? | Call 999, tell him to float, throw something | Jump straight in to pull him out | Run home and say nothing | Climb down and grab his hair
exercise: Zara sees a boy on an inflatable blowing out to sea. Who does she call? | 999 and ask for the Coastguard | The ice-cream van on the promenade | Nobody, he'll float back in by himself | Her friends on the class group chat
exercise: Ethan walks far out on a sandbank. What can the tide do? | Fill the channels behind him and cut him off | Always come in slowly, where he can watch it coming | Go out further the longer he stays | Stop coming in when people are there
exercise: Aanya's nan: «no swimming for an hour after lunch». What matters more? | Flags, cold water and currents | The size of the sandwich she just ate | Waiting exactly 60 minutes after eating | Eating only crisps and no proper lunch
exercise: A rip moves about 2 m a second. How far out does it carry you in 30 seconds? | About 60 m | About 32 m | About 15 m | About 2 m
test: «them flags are for babies, the good waves are over here». What would happen to Kai outside the flags? | He could be in a rip with no lifeguard watching | The waves would be safer there | Lifeguards watch every part of the beach equally | Nothing, flags are just for decoration
test: «rips drag you under, everyone knows». Right? | No, they pull you out, and tiredness and cold drown people | Yes, they pull you to the seabed | Yes, but only at night | No, they push you onto rocks
test: Jayden: «it's giving Maldives, no cap» («no cap» = no lie) at the blue quarry. What's the truth? | The blue hides deep, cold, dangerous water | Blue water means clean and safe | Quarries are warm like holiday seas in the summer | Quarries are shallow at the edges
test: Mia: «if Ollie was drowning he'd be screaming, wouldn't he?» What should she do? | Shout for an adult now; drowning is often silent | Wait until he starts screaming | Swim out and check on him | Film it in case he's messing
test: «I'm a strong swimmer, I'll just jump in for him, sorted». What would likely happen? | Two people in trouble instead of one | The rescue would be quick and easy | The cold water wouldn't affect a good swimmer | The friend would climb out on his own back
test: «It's lush and sunny, the lake'll be like a bath». What's true in the UK? | Sunny air doesn't warm deep water much | Lakes are hottest of all on bright sunny days | Lake water is always above 20 °C in summer | The sun heats a deep lake in a few minutes
test: In a cartoon the hero swims out to sea, punches a wave and swims back easily. What's the real rule? | Swim between the flags; never fight a rip | Punching waves makes them smaller | Strong kids can swim anywhere at sea if they're brave | Waves only hurt cartoon characters
test: Seán: «ah, it's grand, only up to me knees». What can fast water do? | Knock him over even below the knee | Nothing below the knee is dangerous | Push him gently back to the bank | Carry the ball back to him
test: Zara: «the lifeguards have gone home, who do I even ring?» | 999, and ask for the Coastguard | The lifeguards' home phone number | The council office in the morning | Nobody, it's too late to do anything
test: Ethan: «tide's miles away, chill». Why can that change fast? | Tide fills flat sand faster than you walk | Tides only move once a week, so there's lots of time | The tide always comes in from the front | Sandbanks rise with the tide
test: Priya: «bruv, I can't touch the bottom!» and thrashes. What would floating do? | Keep her face up while the gasping passes | Make her sink faster | Make the cold water shock even worse and longer | Pull her into a rip
exam: Identify the principal hazard associated with entering the sea outside the area demarcated by red and yellow flags. | Rip currents in water not supervised by lifeguards | The risk of losing personal belongings | An increased risk of sunburn alone | The presence of excessively warm water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the behaviour of a person who is drowning? | The person is frequently silent and unable to call for assistance | The person invariably screams and waves | The person swims rapidly towards the shore | The person is readily identifiable by splashing
exam: It is given that a companion has entered a canal and is in difficulty. State the conduct recommended by the Royal Life Saving Society. | Call the emergency services, instruct them to float and throw a buoyant object | Enter the water forthwith to effect a rescue | Depart the scene and inform no person | Await the arrival of the companion at the bank
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning cold water shock. | Uncontrolled gasping which subsides within approximately ninety seconds | A pleasant sensation of warmth upon entry | A condition occurring only in water below freezing | A condition prevented by a substantial lunch
